# Assurance policy: production template.  NOT enabled by default.
#
# Copy to a file ending in .rules (never .spocp or .bin), edit, and point
#   wallet_provider.attestation.assurance.rules_file
# at it. Validate with:  assurance-lint -rules <file>
#
# Format: advanced form, ONE RULE PER LINE, each rule preceded by a
# "# id: <name>" comment line. Ids are unique and appear in audit logs.
#
# Rules grant tiers by naming them: (tier (* set a b)) permits every listed
# tier, there is no implicit "at or below". There is no negation: deny is the
# absence of a rule. The floor level is always granted.
#
# Slot order is FIXED (schema version 2) because lists match by position:
#   (assure-ks (tier T)(evidence
#       (verified (fido2 (mds3)(aaguid)(uv)(cert))(platform (source)(app_id))(hsm (id))
#           (instance_key (kind)(home)(level)(boot)(binding)))
#       (claimed (plugin)(key_storage)(user_authentication)(certification))))
# Write a prefix of the slots; use the bare empty list, e.g. (platform), as
# "don't care". Only the (verified ...) group is backend-verified evidence;
# (claimed ...) is whatever the client said.
#
# Replace the placeholder AAGUIDs with the authenticator models you accept.

# id: prod-fido2-mds3-ks
(assure-ks (tier (* set basic enhanced-basic moderate))(evidence (verified (fido2 (mds3 trusted)(aaguid (* set 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002))))))

# User authentication (assure-ua) needs proof that the authenticator verified
# the user (PIN or biometric) when the key was created: (uv present). The
# backend records the user-verified flag of the verified FIDO2 attestation.
# With several keys in a batch uv is "present" only if EVERY key has it.
# The rule below grants moderate only to the listed models AND only with uv;
# a key made without user verification stays at the floor.

# id: prod-fido2-mds3-ua
(assure-ua (tier (* set basic enhanced-basic moderate))(evidence (verified (fido2 (mds3 trusted)(aaguid (* set 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001))(uv present)))))

# ---- Wallet Instance Attestation (WIA) -------------------------------------
#
# K (key storage, above) and S (WIA attestation coverage, below) are
# orthogonal (CS-04 Annex C.2.3): nothing here derives a key storage level
# from the platform, and nothing above derives an S profile from a key.
#
# assure-wia-issue: may a WIA be issued at all? Deny is the absence of a
# rule, so WITHOUT a rule like this one every WIA request is refused. This
# rule permits every request, which is what the backend does with the policy
# disabled. Narrow the evidence to restrict who gets a WIA, for example
#   (evidence (verified (fido2)(platform (source platform_attested))))
# to issue only to wallets that passed native platform attestation.

# id: wia-issue-all
(assure-wia-issue (evidence))

# assure-wia: the S profile. Profiles are probed highest first; the scheme
# floor (s3-backend-only) is what remains. The platform slot is the native
# attestation source verified in THIS request (platform_attested after an
# Apple App Attest / Play Integrity check, development_attested for a
# development App Attest chain, backend_attested when the wallet sent none).

# id: wia-s1-platform
(assure-wia (profile s1-full-client)(evidence (verified (fido2)(platform (source platform_attested)))))

# Optional S1 template that also requires VERIFIED evidence about where the
# wallet's instance key (the WIA PoP key) lives, instead of trusting the
# platform attestation alone. The (instance_key ...) slot is filled only from
# per-key evidence the backend verified for that very key (kind android_key,
# or fido2); the client's claims can never populate it. (kind) is "don't
# care" but must stand in position 1 because slots match by position. Until
# the backend verifies instance-key evidence of kind android_key the slot is
# (instance_key none) and this rule does not match, so leave it commented
# unless your wallets submit such evidence. Uncomment, put a
# "# id: wia-s1-instance-key" line before it, and drop or narrow the plain
# wia-s1-platform rule above if S1 must require it:
#
# (assure-wia (profile s1-full-client)(evidence (verified (fido2)(platform (source platform_attested))(hsm)(instance_key (kind)(home (* set strongbox tee))))))

# Optional S1 template for iOS wallets: the instance key carries an App Attest
# ASSERTION. It requires binding assertion, which means "an attested app
# instance (an App Attest key Apple attested for this app) endorsed this
# instance key". It is NOT evidence that the key lives in the Secure Enclave and
# it is never a hardware attestation, which is why it is a separate, explicit
# rule and why no assure-ks or assure-ua rule may be built on instance_key.
# Slots match by position, so the bare (home)(level)(boot) hold their places.
# The platform slot is a bare "don't care" on purpose: a renewal WIA carries no
# native attestation object (the App Attest key was attested once, earlier), so
# its platform source is backend_attested; the assertion itself is what proves
# an attested app instance is behind this request.
# Uncomment, put a "# id: wia-s1-app-attest-assertion" line before it, and drop
# or narrow the plain wia-s1-platform rule above if S1 must require it:
#
# (assure-wia (profile s1-full-client)(evidence (verified (fido2)(platform)(hsm)(instance_key (kind app_attest_assertion)(home)(level)(boot)(binding assertion)))))

# assure-wia-cert: selects a wallet_solution_certification_information entry
# from the wia_certification catalogue (none: the static
# wallet_provider.wia.certification_info is used). Example, uncomment after
# adding an entry named "wallet-cert-s1" to the catalogue (put a
# "# id: <name>" line before it):
#
#   (rule id would be: wia-cert-s1)
# (assure-wia-cert (name wallet-cert-s1)(evidence (verified (fido2)(platform (source platform_attested)))))
